{"id":1044713,"date":"2026-09-04T12:48:59","date_gmt":"2026-09-04T16:48:59","guid":{"rendered":"https://www.nasa.gov/?p=1044713"},"modified":"2026-09-25T13:16:03","modified_gmt":"2026-09-25T17:16:03","slug":"nasa-hosts-virtual-artemis-webinar-for-blind-low-vision-community","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-hosts-virtual-artemis-webinar-for-blind-low-vision-community/","title":{"rendered":"NASA Hosts Virtual Artemis Webinar for Blind, Low-Vision Community"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1365\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Two technicians at NASA’s Michoud Assembly Facility in New Orleans stand around one of the four RS-25 engines for the agency’s SLS (Space Launch System) rocket on Sept. 8, 2023. The RS-25 engine looks like a bell-shaped nozzle attached to a network of pipes. The engine is being lifted by the horizontal engine installer so it appears to be lying on its side. The RS-25 engine is about the size of a large pickup truck. The technicians are wearing hard hats and safety harnesses.\" style=\"transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg 7138w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=768,512 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=1024,683 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=1536,1024 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=2048,1365 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=400,267 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=600,400 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=900,600 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=1200,800 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blindlowvision.jpg?resize=2000,1333 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">Two technicians at NASA’s Michoud Assembly Facility in New Orleans stand around one of the four RS-25 engines for the agency’s SLS (Space Launch System) rocket on Sept. 8, 2023. The RS-25 engine looks like a bell-shaped nozzle attached to a network of pipes. The engine is being lifted by the horizontal engine installer so it appears to be lying on its side. The RS-25 engine is about the size of a large pickup truck. The technicians are wearing hard hats and safety harnesses.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">Credit: NASA/Michael Democker</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em><strong>Editor&#8217;s Note: To RSVP, individuals also can use the following shortened URL: go.nasa.gov/artemiswebinar. </strong></em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NASA will host a virtual webinar at 2 p.m. EDT on Friday, Oct. 2, titled “The&nbsp;RS-25&nbsp;Engine and the Future of Artemis Missions: An Accessible Webinar for the Blind and Low-Vision Community.” This webinar is open to the public, however it is tailored specifically for a blind and low-vision audience.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The&nbsp;webinar&nbsp;will last about two hours and include an audio-described video of an RS-25 engine test, a Q&amp;A session with an Artemis engineer, and a panel about accessibility in space and science.&nbsp;The event will be hosted on the Zoom platform.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Participants in the session include:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Dr. Kimberly Arcand, visualization scientist, NASA&#8217;s Chandra X-ray Observatory</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Josh Greiner, test director, NASA&#8217;s Stennis Space Center in Bay St. Louis, Mississippi</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Dr. Craig Moore, materials engineer, NASA&#8217;s Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Dr. Robert Shelton, lead simulation engineer, NASA&#8217;s Johnson Space Center in Houston</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Christine Malec, freelance writer and consultant</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Those interested in attending the webinar must RSVP using <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeAgtokLIPaZ-FCpdjz0JPtBrTOmKu5xwjYPz4hGg3MKrJxzg/viewform\" rel=\"noopener\">this form</a> by Friday, Sept. 25.&nbsp;Any questions can be directed to <a href=\"mailto:thalia.k.patrinos@nasa.gov\">thalia.k.patrinos@nasa.gov</a>. The details of the webinar will be emailed to registrants in the days leading up to the event.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NASA’s Artemis program will send astronauts on increasingly difficult missions to explore the Moon and establish a Moon Base on the lunar surface. For additional information on the Artemis missions, visit: </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/artemis\"><strong>https://www.nasa.gov/artemis</strong></a></p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"nasa-gb-align-full width-full maxw-full padding-x-3 padding-y-0 article_a hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-credits-and-details\">\n<section class=\"padding-x-0 padding-top-5 padding-bottom-2 de